# RUN OUTPUT NEVER ENTERS GIT. Not traces, not manifests, not archives.
#
# The old rule was "traces are large so they stay out; manifests are ~2KB of evidence and go
# in", with `!runs/**/*.manifest.json` un-ignoring them. That rule was written when every run
# was against the synthetic corpus, and it then needed a patch (`runs/*_1168*/`) to keep real
# patients out — which made the boundary depend on a DIRECTORY NAMING convention rather than on
# content. A manifest in a directory that happens not to match that glob is committed, and a
# real manifest carries the person_id inside it.
#
# Run output is still an experimental record and must not be deleted — it lives outside the
# repo, under $ACR_LOCAL_ROOT/run/. Deleting it destroys data; committing
# it is a disclosure. Those are different problems and this file only solves the second.
runs/
*.jsonl
*.jsonl.gz

# Generated experiment databases, exports, and visual-QA screenshots stay local. The maintained
# executed notebooks are the portable, redacted walkthrough evidence checked into Git.
artifacts/

# Real corpora must never be committed. Synthetic dev corpus IS committed
# (corpus/patients/) because it is fabricated and PHI-free.
corpus_real/
data_real/

# Env
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# OS / editors
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/

# Reference copy of the prior platform (not ours to redistribute)
_chartreview_ref/

# Second venv for the deepagents runtime
.venv-deep/

# Document-type vocabulary cache, rebuilt on demand (39min -> 10s)
corpus/patients/.doc_type_vocab.json

# Document-type COUNT cache (`corpus.COUNT_CACHE`), written by the corpus itself on first
# `doc_type_counts()`. Ignored by pattern rather than by path: on a real corpus this same file is a
# per-document-type census of patient records, and one committed by accident from a corpus_real
# checkout would be a disclosure. Regenerable either way.
.doc_type_counts.json

# (The real-patient globs that used to live here are gone: `runs/` is ignored wholesale
# above, so there is nothing left for a name-based exception to get wrong.)

# iCloud conflict copies. ~/Desktop is synced, and the corpus generator unlinks and rewrites
# ~5,500 files in a few seconds, which iCloud resolves by leaving "Name 2.txt" beside "Name.txt".
# 1,929 of them appeared during one regeneration. `corpus.FILENAME_RE` does not match the
# space-digit suffix so they never enter a chart and no run was affected — but they are 1,929
# staged additions away from being committed as corpus, and the next reader would have no way
# to tell them from real documents.
#
# THE PATTERN WAS MISSING THE SPACE until 2026-08-03, and what that cost is worth writing down.
# `*[0-9].txt` matches a conflict copy, and it also matches EVERY real document, because a chart
# filename is `Doc-Type_YYYY-MM-DD.txt` and ends in a digit. Already-tracked files were unaffected
# (gitignore only governs untracked ones), so the 5,611 documents committed before the pattern
# landed stayed committed and nothing looked wrong. Everything generated AFTER it was silently
# excluded: the six held-out SYNY charts went in with one file each — their `_ground_truth.json` —
# and 1,589 documents were never committed at all.
#
# That inverts the one guarantee the held-out set exists to provide. `run_manifest.chart_hash`
# says of itself that a chart moving under a stable `patient_id` moved because somebody edited it,
# and that this "is exactly what must not happen quietly on a held-out one after a result has been
# scored against it". The charts not in Git were precisely the held-out six, so an edit to one was
# invisible to version control and the freeze rested on the generator's determinism alone.
#
# The narrow pattern below matches the space-digit shape only. Verified: it catches 0 of the 8,154
# real corpus files, and `!*__[0-9].txt` is gone with it — a `__2` sequence suffix never matched
# the space form, so the exception was itself residue of the wrong pattern.
* [0-9].txt
